@* MobileProductDetail.razor — mobile version of the ProductDetail page.
   Route: /m/Products/{ProductId}
   Layout: MobileLayout

   PORTS a mobile-friendly subset of the desktop ProductDetail.razor
   (2277 lines). The desktop page is a hybrid view + staff management
   surface (photo editor, name/description/price/category/stock edits,
   per-group purchase-limits editor). MOBILE deliberately drops ALL
   staff management affordances — they belong on the dedicated
   /m/Admin/Products surface, not on the customer-facing detail page.
   What mobile keeps + enhances:

     - Page header with back button + product name + category path
       subtitle (breadcrumb-style — the subtitle shows the
       Category › SubCategory › SubSubCategory hierarchy).
     - Hero image (large product picture, or an SVG placeholder when
       PictureUrl is null/empty). Mirrors MobileProducts's picture
       placeholder style.
     - Price + stock badge row — big Toman-formatted price + a stock
       badge (Out / Low / In) using the SAME threshold rule as
       MobileProducts's grid card (≤0 → Out, ≤10 → Low, else In) so
       a product reads the same on the grid and the detail page.
     - Category breadcrumb — horizontal scrollable row containing
       "Products" link (→ /m/Products) + "/" + the Category › Sub ›
       SubSub path. Reuses the desktop ProductDetail.razor hierarchy
       computation verbatim.
     - Long description (when present).
     - Optional salary-budget mini-card (above the Add button) when
       the customer has an active salary budget (i.e. the cart's
       BudgetInfo is populated). Mirrors MobileProducts's .m-budget-card
       so the customer sees their remaining monthly spend before they
       choose a quantity.
     - Qty selector + Add-to-cart — the SAME pattern as MobileProducts:
       .m-qty-selector with − value + buttons, .m-add-btn Add button.
       Disable rules mirror MobileProducts exactly:
         - stock out (product.StockQuantity ≤ 0)
         - salary budget reached (line total > budget.Remaining)
         - purchase limit reached (remaining cap ≤ 0)
         - read-only user (Roles.ReadOnly without shopping roles)
       After successful add: Toast.Success + NavigateTo(Uri, forceLoad:true)
       so MobileLayout's cart bar + BottomNav badge refresh.
     - Loading → error → empty → content state machine (matches the
       MobileProducts / MobileSaleDetail gold-standard pattern).
     - .m-footer with Loc["Version"].

   BACKEND CONTRACT (mirrors desktop ProductDetail.razor):
     - Load: GetProductByIdQuery { ProductId } → Result<ProductDto>.
       [RequireAuthentication] — any authenticated user can view any
       active product. The handler scopes purchase limits to the
       caller's role (admins see all limits; non-admins see only their
       own group's limit reflected as ProductDto.MyPurchaseLimit).
     - Cart load: GetActiveCartForUserQuery → Result<CartDto?>.
       Used to compute "remaining" qty caps (purchase limit + stock
       already in cart) and to populate the optional budget card.
     - Add to cart: CreateOrAppendSaleCommand(ProductId, Quantity) →
       Result<Guid>. [RequireRoles(Customer, Employee, Manager, Admin)]
       — ReadOnly is NOT in the list, so the backend rejects dispatch
       from read-only users as defense-in-depth (the UI also disables
       the button via the _isReadOnly flag).

   ROLE-RESOLUTION PATTERN (matches MobileProducts.razor):
     _isReadOnly is computed in OnInitializedAsync via
     ICurrentUserService.IsInRole (the same source MobileProducts uses
     — different from MobileSaleDetail's AuthenticationStateProvider
     pattern because the role check is structurally identical to the
     gold-standard mobile shop page, NOT a staff-action gate).

   MONEY: FormatMoney mirrors MobileProducts.razor's FormatPrice helper
   (IRR / 10 → Toman, label "تومان" via Loc["TomanUnit"], delegates to
   CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits for the digit swap, returns "—" for
   null/empty). This means the detail page's price reads the SAME as the
   grid page's price — consistent UX.

   TIMEZONE: No timestamps displayed on this page. No FormatTimestamp
   helper needed.

   ANTIFORGERY: Not needed — no EditForm. All buttons are regular
   <button> elements with async @onclick handlers that dispatch
   commands via Wolverine. The Blazor circuit's anti-forgery token is
   established at circuit start. No form POST happens.

   ROUTE PARAMETER:
     `ProductId` is a Guid bound from the URL via [Parameter]. The @page
     constraint `{ProductId:guid}` validates the URL segment is parseable
     as a Guid before this component even loads — a malformed URL gets a
     404 from the router, not a runtime exception here. The route param
     name is `ProductId` (matching the desktop query's `ProductId`
     property name for symmetry — the desktop page uses `Id` on the
     route but the underlying query field is `ProductId`).
*@

@page "/m/Products/{ProductId:guid}"
@attribute [Authorize]
@layout TakOne.WebUI.Components.Layout.MobileLayout.MobileLayout

@using System.Globalization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Localization
@using Wolverine
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Authorization
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Interfaces
@using TakOne.Application.Products.DTOs
@using TakOne.Application.Products.Queries.GetProductById
@using TakOne.Application.Categories.DTOs
@using TakOne.Application.Categories.Queries.GetCategoryByIdWithHierarchy
@using TakOne.Application.Sales.DTOs
@using TakOne.Application.Sales.Queries.GetActiveCartForUser
@using TakOne.Application.Sales.Commands.CreateOrAppendSale
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.Common
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.DTOs
@using TakOne.WebUI.Services

@inject IMessageBus MessageBus
@inject ICurrentUserService CurrentUser
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject IStringLocalizer<MobileProductDetail> Loc
@inject ToastService Toast
@inject AuthenticationStateProvider AuthStateProvider

<PageTitle>@(_product is null ? Loc["PageTitle"] : $"{_product.Name} — TakOne")</PageTitle>

@if (_isLoading)
{
    @* Skeleton: back-header + image hero + price row + breadcrumb +
       description + actions. Mirrors real ProductDetail layout. *@
    <div class="m-page-header-back">
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 2.5rem; height: 2.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"></div>
        <div class="m-page-header-text" style="flex: 1;">
            <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line medium"></div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="m-product-image-hero">
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 100%; height: 100%; border-radius: 0;"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="m-product-detail-price-row">
        <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line long" style="height: 1.5rem;"></div>
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 3rem; height: 1.5rem; border-radius: 0.25rem;"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="m-breadcrumb">
        @for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++)
        {
            <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 4rem; height: 1rem; border-radius: 0.25rem;"></div>
        }
    </div>
    <div class="m-product-detail-description">
        <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line long"></div>
        <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line long" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"></div>
        <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line medium" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="m-product-detail-actions">
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 5rem; height: 2.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"></div>
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 8rem; height: 2.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"></div>
    </div>
    <span class="sr-only">@Loc["Loading"]</span>
}
else if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_loadError))
{
    <div class="m-error-box">
        <p>@_loadError</p>
        <button class="m-btn m-btn-secondary" @onclick="LoadProductAsync">@Loc["RetryButton"]</button>
    </div>
}
else if (_product is null)
{
    @* Not-found / not-allowed: the handler returns Result.Failure with a
       generic "not found" message for both genuinely-missing products AND
       inactive products that customers shouldn't see (defense-in-depth —
       never leak that the product exists). Either way the user sees the
       same friendly ProductNotFound message + a Back-to-Products button. *@
    <div class="m-empty">
        <p>@Loc["ProductNotFound"]</p>
        <button class="m-btn m-btn-secondary" @onclick="GoToProducts">@Loc["BackToProducts"]</button>
    </div>
}
else
{
    var remaining = GetRemaining();
    var limitReached = remaining <= 0;
    var stockOut = _product.StockQuantity <= 0;
    var budget = _cart?.BudgetInfo;
    var unitPrice = _product.Price?.Amount ?? 0m;
    var lineTotal = unitPrice * _qty;
    var salaryReached = budget is not null && lineTotal > budget.Remaining;
    var addDisabled = limitReached || stockOut || salaryReached || _isReadOnly || _isBusy;

    @* ── 1. Page header — back button + product name + category-path
           subtitle. The .m-page-header-back wrapper + .m-back-btn +
           .m-page-header-text classes are mobile-detail-specific (also
           used by MobileSaleDetail) — the orchestrator adds them to
           mobile.css in a later pass (until then the page renders
           unstyled-but-functional). *@
    <div class="m-page-header-back">
        <button type="button"
                class="m-back-btn"
                aria-label="@Loc["BackToProducts"]"
                title="@Loc["BackToProducts"]"
                @onclick="GoToProducts">
            <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" style="width: 1.25rem; height: 1.25rem;">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" />
            </svg>
        </button>
        <div class="m-page-header-text">
            <h1 class="m-page-title">@_product.Name</h1>
            <p class="m-page-subtitle">
                @(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_categoryHierarchy) ? Loc["NoCategory"] : _categoryHierarchy)
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* ── 2. Hero image — large product picture (or an SVG placeholder when
           PictureUrl is null/empty). The .m-product-image base class is
           sized for the small grid-card image (5rem square) — on the
           detail page we add the .m-product-image-hero modifier to make
           it span the full content width with a 16:9-ish aspect ratio
           (orchestrator adds the modifier class to mobile.css in a later
           pass; until then the hero renders at the small card size —
           unstyled-but-functional). The placeholder SVG mirrors
           MobileProducts's photo-icon style so the empty state reads the
           same on the grid and the detail page. *@
    <div class="m-product-image m-product-image-hero">
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_product.PictureUrl))
        {
            <img src="@_product.PictureUrl" alt="@_product.Name" loading="lazy" />
        }
        else
        {
            <svg width="64" height="64" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" style="color: var(--tm-gray);">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M2.25 15.75l5.159-5.159a2.25 2.25 0 013.182 0l5.159 5.159m-1.5-1.5l1.409-1.409a2.25 2.25 0 013.182 0l2.909 2.909m-18 3.75h16.5a1.5 1.5 0 001.5-1.5V6a1.5 1.5 0 00-1.5-1.5H3.75A1.5 1.5 0 002.25 6v12a1.5 1.5 0 001.5 1.5z" />
            </svg>
        }
        @if (stockOut)
        {
            <span class="m-stock-badge m-stock-out">@Loc["OutOfStock"]</span>
        }
        else if (_product.StockQuantity <= 10)
        {
            <span class="m-stock-badge m-stock-low">@Loc["LowStock"]</span>
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="m-stock-badge m-stock-in">@Loc["InStock"]</span>
        }
    </div>

    @* ── 3. Price + stock badge row — big Toman-formatted price (FormatMoney
           mirrors MobileProducts's FormatPrice so the unit reads the same
           on the grid and the detail page) — wait, FormatMoney here is
           semantically the same as MobileProducts's FormatPrice (takes a
           MoneyDto, returns a localized "{amount} {unit}" string). The
           stock badge threshold matches MobileProducts (≤0 Out, ≤10 Low,
           else In). The .m-product-detail-price-row class is page-specific
           (orchestrator adds it to mobile.css in a later pass). *@
    <div class="m-product-detail-price-row">
        <span class="m-product-detail-price">@FormatMoney(_product.Price)</span>
    </div>

    @* ── 4. Category breadcrumb — horizontal scrollable row. The "Products"
           link goes to the mobile grid (/m/Products). The category path
           is the same _categoryHierarchy string computed verbatim from
           the desktop ProductDetail.razor LoadCategoryHierarchyAsync
           helper (Category › SubCategory › SubSubCategory). *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_categoryHierarchy))
    {
        <nav class="m-breadcrumb" aria-label="breadcrumb">
            <a href="/m/Products" class="m-breadcrumb-link">@Loc["BreadcrumbProducts"]</a>
            <span class="m-breadcrumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
            <span class="m-breadcrumb-current">@_categoryHierarchy</span>
        </nav>
    }

    @* ── 5. Long description — shown only when the product has a non-empty
           Description. White-space pre-wrap preserves any intentional
           line breaks the staff wrote in the description textarea. *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_product.Description))
    {
        <p class="m-product-detail-description">@_product.Description</p>
    }

    @* ── 6. Optional salary budget mini-card — shown only when the customer
           has an active salary budget (BudgetInfo is populated by the
           cart-query handler via ISalaryBudgetService). Mirrors
           MobileProducts's .m-budget-card so the budget reads the same
           on the grid and the detail page. The card shows the REMAINING
           amount (Salary.Amount - Consumed) divided by 10 for IRR (→
           Toman), plus a "Used: X%" progress bar when the consumed
           percent is > 0. *@
    @if (budget is not null && budget.Salary.Amount > 0m)
    {
        var consumedPercent = Math.Min(1m, budget.Consumed / budget.Salary.Amount);
        <div class="m-budget-card">
            <div class="m-budget-content">
                <div class="m-budget-info">
                    <p class="m-budget-label">@Loc["BudgetRemaining"]</p>
                    <p class="m-budget-amount">@FormatBudgetAmount()</p>
                    @if (consumedPercent > 0)
                    {
                        <div class="m-budget-bar-container">
                            <div class="m-budget-bar-info">
                                <span>@Loc["BudgetUsed"]</span>
                                <span>@CultureFormat.FormatPercent(consumedPercent, 0)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="m-budget-bar-track">
                                <div class="m-budget-bar-fill" style="width: @($"{consumedPercent:P0}")"></div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <div class="m-budget-icon">
                    <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" style="width: 1.75rem; height: 1.75rem;">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M2.25 8.25h19.5M2.25 9h19.5m-16.5 5.25h6m-6 2.25h3m3.75 0h7.5M3 5.25h18a.75.75 0 01.75.75v12a.75.75 0 01-.75.75H3a.75.75 0 01-.75-.75V6a.75.75 0 01.75-.75z" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    @* ── 7. Qty selector + Add to cart — reuses the EXACT pattern from
           MobileProducts.razor (the gold-standard mobile shop pattern).
           .m-qty-selector with − value + buttons; .m-add-btn Add button
           with a cart-plus icon. Disable rules mirror MobileProducts:
             - read-only user → disabled button labelled "Read only"
             - limit reached (remaining ≤ 0, not stockOut) → "Max reached"
             - salary budget reached (lineTotal > Remaining, not stockOut)
               → "Budget reached"
             - stock out → "Out of stock"
             - otherwise → enabled Add button with cart-plus icon
           The Add button click dispatches CreateOrAppendSaleCommand
           (ProductId, _qty); on success, Toast.Success + reload via
           Navigation.NavigateTo(Uri, forceLoad:true) so MobileLayout's
           cart bar + BottomNav badge refresh. On failure, Toast.Error
           with the handler's error message (or a generic fallback). *@
    <div class="m-product-detail-actions">
        <div class="m-qty-selector">
            <button type="button" class="m-qty-btn"
                    disabled="@(_qty <= 1 || _isReadOnly)"
                    @onclick="DecrementQty">
                −
            </button>
            <span class="m-qty-value">@CultureFormat.FormatDigits(_qty)</span>
            <button type="button" class="m-qty-btn"
                    disabled="@(_qty >= GetQtyMax() || _isReadOnly)"
                    @onclick="IncrementQty">
                +
            </button>
        </div>

        @if (_isReadOnly)
        {
            <button type="button" class="m-add-btn m-add-btn-full" disabled
                    title="@Loc["AddButtonReadOnlyTitle"]">
                @Loc["AddButtonReadOnly"]
            </button>
        }
        else if (limitReached && !stockOut)
        {
            <button type="button" class="m-add-btn m-add-btn-full" disabled
                    title="@Loc["AddButtonLimitReachedTitle"]">
                @Loc["AddButtonLimitReached"]
            </button>
        }
        else if (salaryReached && !stockOut)
        {
            <button type="button" class="m-add-btn m-add-btn-full" disabled
                    title="@Loc["AddButtonSalaryReachedTitle"]">
                @Loc["AddButtonSalaryReached"]
            </button>
        }
        else if (stockOut)
        {
            <button type="button" class="m-add-btn m-add-btn-full" disabled
                    title="@Loc["AddButtonStockOutTitle"]">
                @Loc["AddButtonStockOut"]
            </button>
        }
        else
        {
            <button type="button" class="m-add-btn"
                    disabled="@_isBusy"
                    @onclick="HandleAddToCartAsync">
                @if (_isBusy)
                {
                    <RadzenProgressBarCircular ShowValue="false" Mode="ProgressBarMode.Indeterminate" Size="ProgressBarCircularSize.Small" />
                }
                else
                {
                    <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2.5" style="width: 16px; height: 16px;">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M2.25 3h1.386c.51 0 .955.343 1.087.835l.383 1.437M5.25 6.773l1.922 7.227m-1.922-7.227L5.99 9.75m-.75-3.027A3.003 3.003 0 003.99 3.75H2.25m2.25 0L5.99 9.75M5.25 6.773L4.5 21m1.5-14.227l1.922 7.227M5.99 9.75l1.5 5.625m-1.5-5.625L4.5 21m2.25-5.625l1.409-1.409a2.25 2.25 0 013.182 0l2.909 2.909M16.5 21V9.75M16.5 21l3.75-3.75M16.5 21l-3.75-3.75" />
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4" />
                    </svg>
                    <span>@Loc["AddToCart"]</span>
                }
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @* ── 8. My purchase limit hint — shown only when the customer has a
           per-product purchase limit set for their group (MyPurchaseLimit
           is non-null). Mirrors the desktop ProductDetail.razor's hint
           pattern: "Max {0} per order for your account." Helps the
           customer understand why the qty + button stops at a certain
           number before they hit the cap. *@
    @if (_product.MyPurchaseLimit.HasValue)
    {
        <p class="m-purchase-limit-hint">
            @string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["MyPurchaseLimitHint"], CultureFormat.FormatDigits(_product.MyPurchaseLimit.Value))
        </p>
    }

    @* ── 9. Footer — version string. .m-footer is a global mobile class
           (already in mobile.css). Version is "TakOne v1.0" in en-US and
           "تک‌وان نسخه ۱.۰" in fa-IR (Persian digits — matches the
           convention used in MobileSales / MobileSettings / MobileSaleDetail). *@
    <div class="m-footer">
        <p>@Loc["Version"]</p>
    </div>
}

@code {
    // ── Route parameter ────────────────────────────────────────────────

    [Parameter]
    public Guid ProductId { get; set; }

    // ── State ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// The loaded product. Null while loading or when the load failed.
    /// </summary>
    private ProductDto? _product;

    /// <summary>
    /// The product's category hierarchy string, computed verbatim from
    /// the desktop ProductDetail.razor LoadCategoryHierarchyAsync helper
    /// (Category › SubCategory › SubSubCategory). Empty when the product
    /// has no Category assigned or the hierarchy lookup failed (non-
    /// fatal — the breadcrumb + subtitle gracefully omit the path).
    /// </summary>
    private string? _categoryHierarchy;

    /// <summary>
    /// The current user's active shopping cart (Draft sale). Used to
    /// compute "remaining" qty caps (stock already in cart + purchase
    /// limit) and to populate the optional salary budget mini-card
    /// (BudgetInfo). Null when the user has no active draft or the load
    /// failed (non-fatal — the Add button still works without the cap,
    /// the backend re-checks server-side).
    /// </summary>
    private CartDto? _cart;

    private bool _isLoading = true;
    private string _loadError = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// Selected quantity in the qty selector. Starts at 1. Clamped to
    /// [1, GetQtyMax()] by the +/- buttons.
    /// </summary>
    private int _qty = 1;

    /// <summary>
    /// Add-to-cart in-flight guard. Disables the Add button while a
    /// CreateOrAppendSaleCommand is dispatching so the user can't
    /// double-tap and create duplicate cart lines.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isBusy;

    /// <summary>
    /// True when the current user is in the ReadOnly role WITHOUT any
    /// shopping role (Customer/Employee/Manager/Admin). Computed once in
    /// OnInitializedAsync via ICurrentUserService.IsInRole (same source
    /// as MobileProducts.razor). ReadOnly users see the Add button
    /// disabled with a "Read only" label — the backend also rejects
    /// CreateOrAppendSaleCommand via [RequireRoles(Customer, Employee,
    /// Manager, Admin)] as defense-in-depth.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isReadOnly;

    // ── Lifecycle ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            // Role resolution — matches MobileProducts.razor's pattern.
            // ICurrentUserService is safe here because the role check is
            // a UX gate (not a security boundary — the backend
            // [RequireRoles] attribute is authoritative). For interactive
            // Blazor Server scenarios where ICurrentUserService might
            // resolve from a stale HttpContext, the worst case is the
            // button shows as enabled when it should be disabled — the
            // backend still rejects the dispatch.
            var canShop = CurrentUser.IsInRole(Roles.Customer)
                          || CurrentUser.IsInRole(Roles.Employee)
                          || CurrentUser.IsInRole(Roles.Manager)
                          || CurrentUser.IsInRole(Roles.Admin);
            _isReadOnly = CurrentUser.IsInRole(Roles.ReadOnly) && !canShop;

            // Load the product and the cart in parallel — the cart is
            // needed for the budget card + the qty cap computation, the
            // product is the page itself. The category hierarchy lookup
            // depends on the product being loaded first (we need the
            // product's CategoryId) so it's a sequential follow-up.
            await LoadProductAsync();
            await Task.WhenAll(LoadCartAsync(), LoadCategoryHierarchyAsync());
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Defense-in-depth — the inner loaders swallow their own
            // exceptions and surface a friendly _loadError, but if
            // something unexpected happens (e.g. auth state lookup
            // throws), show the generic error instead of crashing the
            // circuit.
            _loadError = Loc["Error_Unexpected"];
        }
        finally
        {
            _isLoading = false;
        }
    }

    // ── Data loading ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Loads the product by Id. Mirrors the desktop ProductDetail.razor
    /// LoadProductAsync helper verbatim. On success, sets _product; on
    /// failure, sets _loadError with the handler's error message (or a
    /// generic fallback). Also resets _qty to 1 so a retry doesn't
    /// carry over a stale quantity from a previous product load.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadProductAsync()
    {
        var query = new GetProductByIdQuery { ProductId = ProductId };
        try
        {
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<ProductDto>>(query);

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                _product = result.Value;
                _qty = 1;
                _loadError = string.Empty;
            }
            else
            {
                _product = null;
                _loadError = result.Error ?? Loc["Error_FailedToLoad"];
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            _product = null;
            _loadError = Loc["Error_Unexpected"];
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Loads the current user's active shopping cart (Draft sale). The
    /// cart provides BudgetInfo (for the salary budget mini-card) and
    /// LineItems (for the "remaining" qty cap computation). Non-fatal —
    /// if the load fails or the user has no active draft, _cart stays
    /// null and the page renders without the budget card + without the
    /// qty cap (the backend still rejects over-cap adds).
    /// Mirrors MobileProducts.razor's LoadCartAsync helper.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadCartAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<CartDto?>>(new GetActiveCartForUserQuery());
            if (result.IsSuccess && result.Value is not null)
            {
                _cart = result.Value;
            }
            else
            {
                _cart = null;
            }
        }
        catch
        {
            _cart = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Loads the product's full category hierarchy (Category › SubCategory
    /// › SubSubCategory) for the breadcrumb + page header subtitle. The
    /// desktop ProductDetail.razor builds this same string from the same
    /// query — ported verbatim (just using _product instead of the local
    /// variable). Non-fatal: if the lookup fails, the breadcrumb simply
    /// omits the category path.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadCategoryHierarchyAsync()
    {
        if (_product is null || _product.CategoryId == Guid.Empty)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            var query = new GetCategoryByIdWithHierarchyQuery { CategoryId = _product.CategoryId };
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<CategoryDto>>(query);

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                var cat = result.Value;
                var parts = new List<string> { cat.Name };

                if (_product.SubCategoryId.HasValue)
                {
                    var sub = cat.SubCategories.FirstOrDefault(s => s.Id == _product.SubCategoryId.Value);
                    if (sub is not null)
                    {
                        parts.Add(sub.Name);

                        if (_product.SubSubCategoryId.HasValue)
                        {
                            var subsub = sub.SubSubCategories.FirstOrDefault(ss => ss.Id == _product.SubSubCategoryId.Value);
                            if (subsub is not null)
                            {
                                parts.Add(subsub.Name);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }

                _categoryHierarchy = string.Join(" › ", parts);
            }
            // Non-fatal.
        }
        catch
        {
            // Swallow — breadcrumb is purely a UI nicety.
        }
    }

    // ── Qty selector + caps (ported from MobileProducts.razor) ────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Computes the remaining purchaseable quantity for this product:
    ///   remaining = min(StockQuantity - inCartQty, MyPurchaseLimit - inCartQty)
    /// Returns 0 when stock is exhausted OR the purchase limit is reached.
    /// Mirrors MobileProducts.razor's GetRemainingForProduct helper, just
    /// for the single loaded product instead of a per-listing lookup.
    /// </summary>
    private int GetRemaining()
    {
        if (_product is null) return 0;

        var inCart = _cart?.LineItems
            .Where(li => li.ProductId == _product.Id)
            .Sum(li => li.Quantity) ?? 0;

        var remainingStock = Math.Max(0, _product.StockQuantity - inCart);

        if (!_product.MyPurchaseLimit.HasValue)
            return remainingStock;

        var remainingLimit = Math.Max(0, _product.MyPurchaseLimit.Value - inCart);
        return Math.Min(remainingStock, remainingLimit);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Computes the maximum selectable quantity in the qty selector.
    /// Caps at:
    ///   1. Stock minus in-cart quantity (so you can't add more than what
    ///      physically remains in inventory).
    ///   2. MyPurchaseLimit minus in-cart quantity (so you can't add more
    ///      than your group's per-product cap allows).
    ///   3. Salary budget remaining divided by unit price (so the qty
    ///      selector stops before the line total would exceed the budget).
    /// Mirrors MobileProducts.razor's GetQtyMaxForProduct helper.
    /// </summary>
    private int GetQtyMax()
    {
        if (_product is null) return 1;

        var remaining = GetRemaining();
        var cap = _product.MyPurchaseLimit.HasValue
            ? Math.Min(remaining, _product.StockQuantity)
            : _product.StockQuantity;
        var max = Math.Max(1, cap);

        var budget = _cart?.BudgetInfo;
        if (budget is not null && _product.Price is { } price)
        {
            var unitPrice = price.Amount;
            if (unitPrice > 0m)
            {
                var salaryMax = (int)Math.Floor(budget.Remaining / unitPrice);
                max = Math.Min(max, Math.Max(1, salaryMax));
            }
        }

        return max;
    }

    private void IncrementQty()
    {
        var max = GetQtyMax();
        if (_qty < max)
        {
            _qty++;
        }
    }

    private void DecrementQty()
    {
        if (_qty > 1)
        {
            _qty--;
        }
    }

    // ── Add to cart (ported from MobileProducts.razor) ─────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Add-to-cart handler. Mirrors MobileProducts.razor's AddToCartAsync
    /// helper, adapted for the single-product case. The handler dispatches
    /// CreateOrAppendSaleCommand(ProductId, _qty) → Result&lt;Guid&gt;
    /// (the Sale Id). On success: Toast.Success + navigate to the current
    /// URI with forceLoad:true so MobileLayout's cart bar + BottomNav
    /// badge refresh. On failure: Toast.Error with the handler's error
    /// message (or a generic fallback).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task HandleAddToCartAsync()
    {
        if (_isBusy || _product is null) return;

        var qty = _qty;
        if (qty < 1)
        {
            await Toast.Warning(Loc["ToastQtyMin"]);
            return;
        }

        var remaining = GetRemaining();
        if (remaining <= 0)
        {
            await Toast.Warning(Loc["ToastLimitReached"]);
            return;
        }
        if (qty > remaining)
        {
            qty = remaining;
            await Toast.Info(string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["ToastQtyClamped"], CultureFormat.FormatDigits(remaining)));
        }

        var budget = _cart?.BudgetInfo;
        if (budget is not null)
        {
            var unitPrice = _product.Price?.Amount ?? 0m;
            var lineTotal = unitPrice * qty;
            if (lineTotal > budget.Remaining)
            {
                await Toast.Warning(Loc["ToastSalaryReached"]);
                return;
            }
        }

        _isBusy = true;
        try
        {
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<Guid>>(
                new CreateOrAppendSaleCommand(_product.Id, qty));

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                await Toast.Success(Loc["AddedToCart"], summary: Loc["AddedToCartTitle"]);
                _qty = 1;
                // Reload the page so MobileLayout's cart bar + BottomNav
                // badge update with the new cart state. forceLoad:true
                // triggers a full page refresh (matches MobileProducts).
                Navigation.NavigateTo(Navigation.Uri, forceLoad: true);
            }
            else
            {
                await Toast.Error(result.Error ?? Loc["Error_AddToCartFailed"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            }
        }
        catch
        {
            await Toast.Error(Loc["Error_AddToCartFailed"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
        }
        finally
        {
            _isBusy = false;
        }
    }

    // ── Navigation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    private void GoToProducts()
        => Navigation.NavigateTo("/m/Products", forceLoad: false);

    // ── Formatting helpers (mirror MobileProducts.razor) ──────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Formats a MoneyDto for display as Toman: IRR is divided by 10 and
    /// labelled with the localized "Toman" unit; other currencies pass
    /// through unchanged with their own currency code. Returns "—" for
    /// null or zero-with-empty-currency amounts (cleaner than "0 تومان"
    /// on an empty price tag).
    ///
    /// Mirrors MobileProducts.razor's FormatPrice helper exactly so the
    /// detail page's price reads the SAME as the grid page's price —
    /// consistent UX between the two pages that link to each other.
    /// </summary>
    private string FormatMoney(MoneyDto? price)
    {
        if (price is null) return "—";
        var isIrr = string.Equals(price.Currency, "IRR", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
        var displayAmount = isIrr ? price.Amount / 10m : price.Amount;
        var amount = CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits(displayAmount.ToString("N0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
        var unit = isIrr ? Loc["TomanUnit"] : price.Currency;
        return $"{amount} {unit}";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Formats the remaining salary budget amount for the budget mini-
    /// card. Mirrors MobileProducts.razor's FormatBudgetAmount helper.
    /// Returns "—" when no budget is loaded (defensive — the card is
    /// only rendered when budget.Salary.Amount > 0, so this fallback is
    /// a dead code path but kept for parity with MobileProducts).
    /// </summary>
    private string FormatBudgetAmount()
    {
        var budget = _cart?.BudgetInfo;
        if (budget is null || budget.Salary.Amount <= 0m) return Loc["NoBudget"];

        var isIrr = string.Equals(budget.Salary.Currency, "IRR", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
        var remaining = isIrr ? budget.Remaining / 10m : budget.Remaining;
        var amount = CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits(Math.Max(0, remaining).ToString("N0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
        var unit = isIrr ? Loc["TomanUnit"] : budget.Salary.Currency;
        return $"{amount} {unit}";
    }
}
